## Root Cause

`ShardReplicaSet::queue_proxify_local` temporarily removes the current local shard from `self.local` with `local.take()`, then awaits queue-proxy construction before reinserting either `Shard::QueueProxy` or the original `Shard::Local`.

This creates a cancellation window:

```text
self.local = Some(Shard::Local(_))
    -> local.take()
self.local = None
    -> await QueueProxyShard::new(...) / new_from_version(...)
    -> future is cancelled
LocalShard is dropped and self.local remains None
```

Analyzed revision: `44ad62f8cd69642be5afa6441612525e24a0d063`.

The risky transition is in `lib/collection/src/shards/replica_set/shard_transfer.rs`:

```rust
// Get `max_ack_version` without "taking" local shard (to maintain cancel safety)
let local_shard = match local.deref() {
    Some(Shard::Local(local)) => local,
    Some(Shard::ForwardProxy(proxy)) => &proxy.wrapped_shard,
    _ => unreachable!(),
};

let wal_keep_from = local_shard
    .update_handler
    .lock()
    .await
    .wal_keep_from
    .clone();

// Proxify local shard
//
// Making `await` calls between `local.take()` and `local.insert(...)` is *not* cancel safe!
let local_shard = match local.take() {
    Some(Shard::Local(local)) => local,
    Some(Shard::ForwardProxy(proxy)) => proxy.wrapped_shard,
    _ => unreachable!(),
};

// Try to queue proxify with or without version
let proxy_shard = match from_version {
    None => {
        Ok(QueueProxyShard::new(local_shard, remote_shard, wal_keep_from, progress).await)
    }
    Some(from_version) => {
        QueueProxyShard::new_from_version(
            local_shard,
            remote_shard,
            wal_keep_from,
            from_version,
            progress,
        )
        .await
    }
};
```

The comment correctly says that awaiting between `local.take()` and reinsertion is not cancel safe, but the next block does exactly that. If the future is cancelled at the queue-proxy construction await, `self.local` has already been set to `None` and no recovery owner remains that can put the `LocalShard` back.

The awaited constructor owns the `LocalShard` while waiting on the WAL lock:

```rust
// lib/collection/src/shards/queue_proxy_shard.rs
pub async fn new(
    wrapped_shard: LocalShard,
    remote_shard: RemoteShard,
    wal_keep_from: Arc<AtomicU64>,
    progress: Arc<ParkingMutex<TransferTaskProgress>>,
) -> Self {
    Self {
        inner: Some(Inner::new(wrapped_shard, remote_shard, wal_keep_from, progress).await),
    }
}
```

```rust
// lib/collection/src/shards/queue_proxy_shard.rs
pub async fn new(
    wrapped_shard: LocalShard,
    remote_shard: RemoteShard,
    wal_keep_from: Arc<AtomicU64>,
    progress: Arc<ParkingMutex<TransferTaskProgress>>,
) -> Self {
    let start_from = wrapped_shard.wal.wal.lock().await.last_index() + 1;
    Self::new_from_version(
        wrapped_shard,
        remote_shard,
        wal_keep_from,
        start_from,
        progress,
    )
}
```

`new_from_version` has the same shape:

```rust
pub async fn new_from_version(
    wrapped_shard: LocalShard,
    remote_shard: RemoteShard,
    wal_keep_from: Arc<AtomicU64>,
    version: u64,
    progress: Arc<ParkingMutex<TransferTaskProgress>>,
) -> Result<Self, (LocalShard, CollectionError)> {
    // Lock WAL until we've successfully created the queue proxy shard
    let wal = wrapped_shard.wal.wal.clone();
    let wal_lock = wal.lock().await;
    ...
}
```

This matters because shard transfers are explicitly cancellable. The transfer driver wraps the transfer future in `cancel::future::cancel_on_token`, which drops the in-progress business future when the cancellation token wins:

```rust
// lib/common/cancel/src/future.rs
pub async fn cancel_on_token<Fut>(
    cancel: CancellationToken,
    future: Fut,
) -> Result<Fut::Output, Error>
where
    Fut: Future,
{
    tokio::select! {
        biased;
        _ = cancel.cancelled() => Err(Error::Cancelled),
        output = future => Ok(output),
    }
}
```

