### Describe the bug

The ClickHouse sink checkpoint path can lose the only local owner of an in-flight ClickHouse insert finalization if the sink consumer future is cancelled while `ClickHouseSinkWriter::barrier(true)` is awaiting `Insert::end()`.

The affected state machine is:

write rows into ClickHouse Insert
    -> checkpoint barrier
    -> self.inserter.take()
    -> await inserter.end()
    -> parent sink-consumer future is dropped/cancelled before end returns
    -> writer.inserter == None and abort() has no finalization owner
    -> sink log is not truncated, so replay cannot know whether ClickHouse applied the first finalization

This violates the external-state consistency invariant expected from a decoupled sink: the connector should either finish the external checkpoint side effect and then truncate the sink log, or cancel before the external side effect can reach an ambiguous committed state. The current code creates a third state where the ClickHouse insert finalization may have been sent or applied, but RisingWave has no surviving owner or durable token to reconcile the result before replay.

