### Summary

Manually driving a connection through `hyper_util::server::conn::auto::Builder::serve_connection_with_upgrades` (instead of `axum::serve`, which exposes no builder-customization hook) resolves `Ok(())` and the handling task ends normally, but the TCP client never observes EOF. A client doing a blocking `read_to_end()` after sending `Connection: close` hangs indefinitely, even though the server has, by all observable signals, finished serving that connection successfully.

### Code Sample

```rust
use axum::routing::get;
use axum::Router;
use hyper_util::rt::{TokioExecutor, TokioIo};
use hyper_util::server::conn::auto::Builder as HyperConnBuilder;
use hyper_util::service::TowerToHyperService;
use tokio::io::{AsyncReadExt, AsyncWriteExt};
use tokio::net::{TcpListener, TcpStream};

#[tokio::test]
async fn repro() {
    let router: Router = Router::new().route("/", get(|| async { "hi" }));

    let listener = TcpListener::bind("127.0.0.1:0").await.unwrap();
    let addr = listener.local_addr().unwrap();

    tokio::spawn(async move {
        loop {
            let (io, _remote_addr) = listener.accept().await.unwrap();
            let io = TokioIo::new(io);
            let router = router.clone();
            let tower_service = tower::service_fn(move |req: axum::extract::Request<hyper::body::Incoming>| {
                let mut router = router.clone();
                async move { tower::Service::call(&mut router, req).await }
            });
            let hyper_service = TowerToHyperService::new(tower_service);
            tokio::spawn(async move {
                let builder = HyperConnBuilder::new(TokioExecutor::new());
                let result = builder.serve_connection_with_upgrades(io, hyper_service).await;
                eprintln!("conn finished: {:?}", result.is_ok()); // prints `true`
            });
        }
    });

    let mut stream = TcpStream::connect(addr).await.unwrap();
    stream
        .write_all(b"GET / HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: 127.0.0.1\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n")
        .await
        .unwrap();

    let mut response = Vec::new();
    stream.read_to_end(&mut response).await.unwrap(); // <-- HANGS here, sometimes

    /** 
     * Important caveat on reproducing this: this exact minimal version, run in isolation 
     * (one `#[tokio::test]`, nothing else in the binary), passes reliably for us across many runs. 
     * 
     * The hang became reliably reproducible only once this same pattern was embedded in our actual 
     * application and exercised by our real integration test suite (3 real-TCP `#[tokio::test]`s 
     * in one test binary, `cargo test` default settings — multiple `current_thread` runtimes, one 
     * per test, all in the same process). We were unable to shrink the reproduction further down to 
     * a small standalone crate before running out of time to investigate — see "Additional Context" 
     * for the most specific signal we captured before stopping.
     */
}
```

### Expected Behavior

After `serve_connection_with_upgrades`'s future resolves as `Ok(())`, the underlying TCP connection should be fully closed (FIN sent) — a client that requested `Connection: close` and is blocked on `read_to_end()` should observe EOF and return.


### Actual Behavior

The connection future resolves `Ok(())`, the per-connection task ends normally, and (in our real application) every log line we'd expect for a fully successful request/response cycle appears in the right order with no error — yet the client-side socket never receives a FIN, and `read_to_end()` hangs indefinitely.


