### Describe the bug

When `xtask bench` downloads a remote asset whose workload entry omits `sha256`, it writes the response directly to the asset's canonical cache pathname. If Ctrl-C cancels the workload after a response prefix has been written but before the body reaches EOF, the download future is dropped and the incomplete canonical file remains.

A later invocation opens that file during the cache lookup. Because `check_sha256` returns `true` when no expected digest is configured, `fetch_assets` treats the incomplete file as a valid cache hit, skips the remote request, and returns success.

```text
missing hashless remote asset
-> open/truncate canonical cache pathname
-> write a response prefix
-> Ctrl-C aborts the workload owner
-> dropping fetch_assets drops its JoinSet and aborts the download child
-> incomplete canonical file remains
-> next run checks the same path with sha256 = None
-> check_sha256 returns true
-> fetch_assets reuses the incomplete file without another request
```

This violates the cache-publication invariant: a canonical asset should become reusable only after the response reaches EOF, buffered writes complete successfully, and all configured validation succeeds. No cancellation point should leave an uncommitted artifact at a pathname that a later invocation interprets as committed.

The source-level chain below is pinned to commit `fff2ef5a42658b16a937d922aabc3fb7f89f2018`.

1. The benchmark workload runs in a spawned owner task, and its `AbortHandle` is passed to the Ctrl-C handler:
   https://github.com/meilisearch/meilisearch/blob/fff2ef5a42658b16a937d922aabc3fb7f89f2018/crates/xtask/src/bench/mod.rs#L114-L169

```rust
let workload_runs = tokio::spawn(async move {
    for workload_file in args.common.workload_file.iter() {
        // ...
        workload::execute(/* ... */).await?;
    }
    Ok::<_, anyhow::Error>(dashboard_urls)
});

let abort_handle = workload_runs.abort_handle();
tokio::spawn({
    let dashboard_client = dashboard_client.clone();
    dashboard_client.cancel_on_ctrl_c(invocation_uuid, abort_handle)
});
```

2. The Ctrl-C path explicitly aborts that owner task:
   https://github.com/meilisearch/meilisearch/blob/fff2ef5a42658b16a937d922aabc3fb7f89f2018/crates/xtask/src/bench/dashboard.rs#L141-L148

```rust
pub async fn cancel_on_ctrl_c(self, invocation_uuid: Uuid, abort_handle: AbortHandle) {
    // ...
    match ctrl_c().await {
        Ok(()) => {
            self.mark_as_failed(invocation_uuid, None).await;
            abort_handle.abort();
        }
        // ...
    }
}
```

3. The workload owner awaits `fetch_assets`, so cancellation can drop it while asset downloads are pending:
   https://github.com/meilisearch/meilisearch/blob/fff2ef5a42658b16a937d922aabc3fb7f89f2018/crates/xtask/src/bench/workload.rs#L90-L103

```rust
pub async fn execute(/* ... */) -> anyhow::Result<()> {
    assets::fetch_assets(assets_client, &workload.assets, &args.common.asset_folder).await?;
    // ...
}
```

4. `fetch_assets` owns its download children in a local `JoinSet` and awaits them. Tokio documents that dropping a `JoinSet` immediately aborts all tasks in the set:
   https://github.com/meilisearch/meilisearch/blob/fff2ef5a42658b16a937d922aabc3fb7f89f2018/crates/xtask/src/common/assets.rs#L77-L147
   https://docs.rs/tokio/1.52.3/tokio/task/struct.JoinSet.html

```rust
let mut download_tasks = tokio::task::JoinSet::new();
// ...
download_tasks.spawn(download_asset(client, name, asset, location, store_filename));
// ...
while let Some(res) = download_tasks.join_next().await {
    res.context("download task panicked")?.context("download task failed")?;
}
```

5. `download_asset` opens and truncates the canonical destination before awaiting the body stream. It has neither a temporary-file publication step nor a cancellation guard that removes an abandoned destination:
   https://github.com/meilisearch/meilisearch/blob/fff2ef5a42658b16a937d922aabc3fb7f89f2018/crates/xtask/src/common/asset

