## Research References

This fix follows well-established patterns from database recovery literature:

### Write-Ahead Logging (WAL) Protocol

The phase guard implements the same principle as the **write-ahead logging protocol** used in transaction processing systems. In WAL, a durable log record is written before the corresponding data modification. Here, the initializing marker is analogous to a log record: it must only persist once the system has reached a point of no return.

- **Mohan, C. et al., "ARIES: A Transaction Recovery Method Supporting Fine-Granularity Locking and Partial Rollbacks Using Write-Ahead Logging," ACM Transactions on Database Systems, Vol. 17, No. 1, 1992.**
  - Section 2.1: The WAL protocol guarantees that log records are durable before any data page is written. Our phase guard ensures the marker is only semantically committed after destructive steps begin.

- **Gray, J. & Reuter, A., "Transaction Processing: Concepts and Techniques," Morgan Kaufmann, 1993.**
  - Chapter 11: The Write-Ahead

Solana validators ingest hundreds of thousands of streams per second during leader rounds (1 stream = 1 transaction). The receive loop looks something like this (pseudo code):

```rust
loop {
  let mut stream = conn.accept_uni().await;
  while stream_has_data {
    stream.read_chunks(&mut chunks).await;        
  }
  // implicit drop(stream)
}
```


So we accept, read a stream until completion, and then drop it.

RecvStream::drop currently acquires the connection state mutex which is quite contended (see profile below).

The solution I'm proposing for this problem is an early return in cases where all data read is true and it would be safe to ignore the remove operation on `conn.blocked_readers` since you cannot have a blocked reader when the all data read is true as tested in https://github.com/Perelyn-sama/quinn/blob/be95075542a318a19d05461c80669e3f12ee0c66/quinn/src/tests.rs#L949 and the error case also does an early drop, so it is safe to ignore as well.

Futhermore, the optimization extends beyond recvstream::drop, since `ConnectionRef` is also part of `RecvStream` and it has a mutex lock in its own drop function, I was still facing contention. My solution is to move the connection reference counter from `connection::State` to `connection::Shared` and wrap it around an `AtomicUsize` so it could be safely operated on in multiple threads. I'm doing this because `ref_count` is the main variable that is needed in `ConnectionRef::clone` and `ConnectionRef::drop` and placing a mutex lock just to access a usize seemed inefficient.

With the changes in this PR, I was able to improve `RecvStream::drop` by 14% in this simple client/server project that reproduces the issue(https://github.com/Perelyn-sama/ales-interlude/blob/master/src/bin/server.rs)

