A connection that is closed while its congestion window is saturated never sends CONNECTION_CLOSE. The peer receives nothing and only finds out via its own idle timeout.

Observed on quinn 0.11.11 / quinn-proto 0.11.16; verified still present on master (148d760d8845dc6315d554a39a1f1f83a4134bfe) with the repro test below.

## Mechanism

1. `Connection::close()` → `close_inner()` calls `close_common()`, which stops **all** timers (including loss detection), then re-arms only `Timer::Close` ([connection/mod.rs#L1251-L1259](https://github.com/quinn-rs/quinn/blob/148d760d8845dc6315d554a39a1f1f83a4134bfe/quinn-proto/src/connection/mod.rs#L1251-L1259), [#L3494-L3499](https://github.com/quinn-rs/quinn/blob/148d760d8845dc6315d554a39a1f1f83a4134bfe/quinn-proto/src/connection/mod.rs#L3494-L3499)).

2. The CONNECTION_CLOSE frame can only be emitted from `poll_transmit`'s per-space loop. That loop computes `ack_eliciting` from `pending` — which still contains the stream retransmit data queued before the close — and then congestion-gates the packet: `ack_eliciting && loss_probes == 0 && in_flight.bytes + bytes_to_send > congestion.window()` → skip the space ([#L595-L616](https://github.com/quinn-rs/quinn/blob/148d760d8845dc6315d554a39a1f1f83a4134bfe/quinn-proto/src/connection/mod.rs#L595-L616)). The packet that would actually be built contains **only ACKs + CONNECTION_CLOSE** ([#L786-L848](https://github.com/quinn-rs/quinn/blob/148d760d8845dc6315d554a39a1f1f83a4134bfe/quinn-proto/src/connection/mod.rs#L786-L848)) — neither is ack-eliciting nor counts as in-flight — but the gate classifies it by the pending frames a closing connection will never send.

3. Nothing can ever unblock the gate:
   - In `State::Closed`, incoming packets are scanned only for `Frame::Close`; **ACK frames are not processed**, so `in_flight` never drains ([#L2475-L2497](https://github.com/quinn-rs/quinn/blob/148d760d8845dc6315d554a39a1f1f83a4134bfe/quinn-proto/src/connection/mod.rs#L2475-L2497)).
   - Loss-detection timers were stopped in step 1, so nothing is declared lost and no loss probes are queued — the `loss_probes != 0` bypass never fires.
   - Each incoming peer packet re-arms `self.close = true` ([#L2451-L2454](https://github.com/quinn-rs/quinn/blob/148d760d8845dc6315d554a39a1f1f83a4134bfe/quinn-proto/src/connection/mod.rs#L2451-L2454)), which just repeats the blocked attempt.

So if `close()` lands while the connection holds ≥ cwnd of unacked ack-eliciting data — routine for a bulk sender that cancels mid-transfer — the CONNECTION_CLOSE datagram is never sent. The closing side drains locally after 3×PTO and the peer sees silence until its idle timeout.

## Real-world impact

We hit this in a file-transfer engine: cancelling a sender mid-transfer left the receiver as a zombie for its full 134 s idle timeout, on every cancel that landed while the window was saturated. Packet captures show zero CONNECTION_CLOSE datagrams leaving the sender.

