# The problem
The current behavior of `#[tokio::main]` is to wait for spawned blocking tasks.

There are good reason for this behavior:
- It matches the default behavior of dropping a `Runtime` object
- It guarantees that after a `#[tokio::main]` decorated function returns, it has dropped all resources

There are also good reasons against this behavior, especially if the `#[tokio::main]` function is used as the actual `main()` function of the program:
- It makes it impossible to schedule a reliable shutdown due to a very unpredictable waiting time after the `main` function
  - This is not an abstract problem, it can happen for example when [`tokio::net::lookup_host()` gets cancelled during a shutdown process](https://github.com/Finomnis/tokio-graceful-shutdown/issues/50).
- It is inconsistend with `std::thread::spawn` and `tokio::spawn`, both of which get killed/cancelled at the end of `main()`

This means that in its current state, `#[tokio::main]` is not usable if a predictable shutdown has to be performed at any point of the program, which I would argue is a somewhat regular usecase.

# The solution I'd like
The solution I'd like most is if `#[tokio::main]` would perform a [`shutdown_background()`](https://docs.rs/tokio/latest/tokio/runtime/struct.Runtime.html#method.shutdown_background) after the `main()` function is finished, as I'd argue that this is the most commonly desired and most intuitive behavior, although I understand that this is not possible as it would be a breaking API behavior change.

So I'd argue that a new flag for `tokio::main` should be introduced to make this an opt-in behavior.

# Alteratives I've considered
To perform a reliable shutdown, only two possible solutions came to my mind:
- Tell people that `#[tokio::main]` isn't compatible with reliable shutdowns
- Introduce a thread that kills main after a grace period, like this:
```rust
use std::{thread, time::Duration};

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    tokio::task::spawn_blocking(|| {
        thread::sleep(Duration::from_secs(10));
    });

    thread::spawn(|| {
        thread::sleep(Duration::from_millis(1000));
        println!("Shutdown seems to hang. Killing program ...");
        std::process::exit(1);
    });
}
```

Both of which are either non-ideal or bad workarounds.

# Additional thoughts

The argument was made that it was discussed in the past that `#[tokio::main]` should not mirror all the options of `Runtime`, and instead `Runtime` should be used directly. I'd argue, though, that an exception should be made in this case, because this scenario is too common to make it incompatible with `#[tokio::main]`.

I'd be open to make a pull request with this change as soon as I get feedback from the devs of whether or not they agree with my view.

The exact name of this flag is of course open for debate.

