**Description**

Dear tokio contributors!

I believe I have found a memory leak in tokio that is caused by future cancellation in a task.

In short, waker object has a strong reference to the task, and the task has the size of the top level future plus some. Even if the future terminates (and is dropped), task memory will not be freed until any clone of the waker object is still alive.

On the one hand, this seems like a niche thing. On the other, for us the stars aligned in a way that one of our services started leaking 20GB a day. With no change to its code, just some change in API usage from the customers' side. Point is, it can be severe.

Longer story:

When using `tokio::select!`, it is possible to run two or more futures concurrently, and this way when one resolves, the other get canceled. There are other ways to reproduce the issue, but the one with select I think is the easiest.

When tokio executor polls tasks, it passes a waker to the top level future, which in turn passes it down the call chain. The leaf futures that do not resolve immediately are capturing this waker (a clone of) to later signal that the future can be polled again.

When select is used in the top level future of a task, and one of the branches captures the waker, and the top level future is canceled, the task memory is not freed until all waker clones are dropped. And I can imagine it can get stuck forever (e.g. awaiting on some data that never arrives).

I've taken a look through the tokio source code, and, although it's a bit overwhelming (and I might be totally wrong in my conclusions), I think the issue boils down to the fact that the task stores
the future in an enum here:

`tokio/tokio/src/runtime/task/core.rs`

```rust
/// Either the future or the output.
pub(super) enum Stage<T: Future> {
    Running(T),
    Finished(super::Result<T::Output>),
    Consumed,
}

```
This enum has the size of at least the size of the future. When the task terminates it flips the enum to the `Consumed` state, and further awakes are noops, but the size of the enum does not change obviously.

I believe this enum is in turn wrapped in a bunch of structures, with `RawTask` being the top. The `RawTask` implements a bespoke version of ref counting. I believe wakers hold a reference here, and thus prevent the `Stage` enum from being dropped.

