A common use case for intercepting ^C is gracefully rolling back partial modifications in critical sections. What I want is roughly:

```rust
let mut critical_section = tokio::spawn(async {
    foo().await;
    bar().await;
    baz().await;
});

tokio::select! {
    _ = &mut critical_section => {
        // operation completed successfully
    }
    _ = tokio::signal::ctrl_c() => {
        // abort as soon as possible
        critical_section.abort();
        let _ = critical_section.await;
        rollback_operations().await;
    }
}
```

This is, of course, racy: if ^C arrives after the critical section started, but before `tokio::signal::ctrl_c()` was called, the operations will not be rolled back. This cannot be resolved by creating the `ctrl_c()` future before starting the critical section, since the docs for `ctrl_c` emphasize that the future also has to be polled:

> Once the returned future is polled, a listener is registered. The future will complete on the first received `ctrl-c` after the initial call to either `Future::poll` or `.await`.

So the correct approach seems to be:

```rust
let mut ctrl_c = pin!(tokio::signal::ctrl_c());
ctrl_c.as_mut().poll(&mut Context::from_waker(Waker::noop()));

let mut critical_section = tokio::spawn(async {
    foo().await;
    bar().await;
    baz().await;
});

tokio::select! {
    _ = &mut critical_section => {
        // operation completed successfully
    }
    _ = ctrl_c => {
        // abort as soon as possible
        critical_section.abort();
        let _ = critical_section.await;
        rollback_operations().await;
    }
}
```

This feels hacky, and I'm not 100% sure if it actually works correctly.

Contrast this with `Notify`, where `Notify::enable` replaces manual polling in a similar context. Would it be reasonable for `ctrl_c` to return a non-opaque future with an `enable` method?

## Idea

MSPC Receiver to have a length_changed() future. 

## When it could be useful.

Sometimes, when draining an mspc queue into an async write interface, like a sink backed by a socket, it is useful to have a view of your backpressure if the `sink.send()` is pending. For example (semi pseudo code): 

```rust
let item = queue.recv().await;

let send_fut = socket.send(item);
pin_mut(send_fut);

loop {
   select! {
    result = &mut send_fut => {
          return Ok(())
    }
    new_queue_size = queue.changed() => {
          if new_queue_size > MAX_BACKPRESSURE {
                return Err("back pressure: socket pending")
          }
    }
  }
}

```

## Possible Solution

It could be implemented like so

```rust
impl<T, S: Semaphore> Rx<T, S> {
     // Not sure where something like this would be exposed Rx is a private struct
    async pub changed(&self) -> usize {
        let mut ran = false;
        poll_fn(|cx| {
            if ran {
                Poll::Ready(self.lenh)
            }

            self.inner.rx_waker.register_by_ref(cx.waker());
            ran = true;
            Poll::Pending
        }).await
    }
}
```

## Alternatives

The number of items held in backpressure could also be put in a vec dequeue, so recv could be ran while socket send is polling, and the loop could continue.  This might be better, but less obvious architecture.

