### Summary

On Unix, the first line read from `Deno.stdin.readable` is intermittently lost (eaten and silently discarded) when reading interactively. It is most reproducible on WSL2, where it fails roughly 1/3 of the time. Once the first read succeeds, every subsequent read works correctly for the rest of the process lifetime, so it is specifically a first-read startup race.

This was reported on Discord with the canonical "read lines from stdin" pattern:

```ts
import { TextLineStream } from "jsr:@std/streams/text-line-stream";
const encoder = new TextEncoder();
const lineStream = Deno.stdin.readable
  .pipeThrough(new TextDecoderStream())
  .pipeThrough(new TextLineStream());

await Deno.stdout.write(encoder.encode("> "));
for await (const line of lineStream) {
  console.log(`=> ${line}`);
  await Deno.stdout.write(encoder.encode("\n> "));
}
```

Observed: the first `hi` you type produces no `=> hi`. Only after typing a second line does a single `=> hi` appear. The first line's bytes are gone, not buffered (you get one output, not two).

### Root cause

The loss is not "skipping", it is an orphaned blocking syscall consuming and discarding input.

1. On Unix, every `Deno.stdin` read runs as a blocking `read(0)` on a `spawn_blocking` thread (`ext/io/lib.rs`, the `StdFileResourceKind::Stdin` arm of `read_byob`).
2. `Deno.stdin.readable` is a cancellable byte stream. Its `cancel()` calls `core.cancelRead(0)` (`ext/web/06_streams.js`).
3. `core.cancelRead` only cancels the JS-side promise. It cannot interrupt a `read(0)` that is already parked in the kernel. The orphaned read stays blocked, consumes the next line the user types, and then discards it because the JS side already moved on. The next live read only sees the line after that.

The Windows arm directly above the Unix arm has dedicated cancellation handling (`handle_stdin_read`, commented "special read cancellation logic for stdin"). The Unix arm has none.

### Why WSL2 and why intermittent

On WSL2, fd 0 ends up in `O_NONBLOCK`. stdin/stdout/stderr on a TTY share a single open file description, and libuv's `uv_tty_init`/`uv_pipe_open` set `O_NONBLOCK` on it, which is per-description and therefore leaks onto fd 0. Deno's own comment documents this in `ext/io/lib.rs`:

```rust
// Stdin may be set to non-blocking mode by Node's process.stdin
// (via uv_pipe_open/uv_tty_init which set O_NONBLOCK on the fd).
// Since O_NONBLOCK is per-file-description, it affects all users
// of fd 0. Retry on WouldBlock to avoid surfacing EAGAIN to JS.
```

Deno then handles the non-blocking fd by busy-spinning on `EAGAIN` with `std::thread::yield_now()`. That spin widens the timing window around the very first read while the stream/pipe is still being set up, which is when a read can get orphaned. Hit the window and the first line is eaten; miss it and it works. That explains the intermittency and why it is always the first read only: once the steady-state loop is running nothing cancels the read, so it is correct from then on.

### Minimal proof of the mechanism

The following isolates the orphan independent of WSL2 and reproduces on a normal blocking TTY too (run under a pty): park a read, cancel it before any input arrives, then read again.

```ts
const enc = new TextEncoder();
const reader = Deno.stdin.readable.getReader();
const p = reader.read(); // parks in read(0)
p.then((r) => {
  const v = r.value ? new TextDecoder().decode(r.value).trim() : "<closed>";
  Deno.stdout.write(enc.encode(`READER_RESOLVED=[${v}]\n`));
});
await Deno.stdout.write(enc.encode("READY\n"));
setTimeout(async () => {
  await reader.cancel(); // -> core.cancelRead(0): orphans the parked read
  await Deno.stdout.write(enc.encode("CANCELLED\n"));
  const buf = new Uint8Array(256);
  const n = await Deno.stdin.read(buf); // fresh read(0)
  const got = n ? new TextDecoder().decode(buf.subarray(0, n)).trim() : "<eof>";
  await Deno.stdout.write(enc.encode(`SECOND_READ=[${got}]\n`));
  Deno.exit(0);
}, 200);
```

