Replacement of https://github.com/qdrant/qdrant/pull/7067 and https://github.com/qdrant/qdrant/pull/7069 since we decided to define fallback shard key on request level instead of collection config. This approach gives more control to the user and allows better scaling since now they can have multiple fallback shards.

Imagine the user starts with the following request and only has a default shard in the beginning so all the points will only be written to `default` shard key:

```http
PUT /collections/test/points
{
  "points": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "payload": {
        "tenant": "orgX"
      },
      "vector": {}
    }
  ],
  "shard_key": {
    "target": "orgX",
    "fallback": "default"
  }
}
```

Eventually if the tenant grows and user wants to migrate this tenant to a dedicated shard key `orgX`, they can create a new shard key `orgX` and then all new points will start getting inserted into `orgX` once it exists without having to change the original request. 


Note that if there are multiple fallback shards, they might have to setup a mapping logic for the fallback. Despite user having to define their shards dynamically in such case, it's still valuable to have the concept of 'fallback' shard because it routes the internal read and write traffic during transfers so they get a consistent view of the data. 

In the next PR, I'll implement logic to transfer points between two shard keys and modify read/write shard routing logic accordingly to read and write from both during the transfer.

## Summary
This PR adds an experimental non-documented feature that allows to drop the unapplied records in the WAL.

It's helpful in cases where the update queue is stuck with tons of unapplied requests.

## Description
PR adds a new HTTP entry `POST /collection/collection_name/drop_wal`. This command removes all WAL entries, which are guaranteed to be non-applied. It performs the operation over the local shard. In distributed systems, this command should be called on each node.

The result is a simple json with an integer with the amount of dropped records in all shards of the collection. It's not a json because of its experimental feature without documentation.

It also has a `wait` flag for the cases, where WAL drop takes a lot of time and may be timeouted (example of a dropping performance is below).

## Security
Not sure if some strict mode checks are required. However, WAL dropping is allowed only for users with `manage` RBAC access.

## Implementation details
Providing the operation from endpoint up to shard is clear.

On the shard level, it's not enough to just clear the WAL. Because the update operations are also stored in the update worker channel, where each operation has a seq num already.

Before locking the WAL, the truncate operation clear first the update worker channel by the following steps:
1. This PR addes a update lock, which can lock sender of the update worker channel. Lock it first
2. This PR addes a skip updates flag, which on receiver skip update operation. Set this flag to true as a second step.
3. Next, send the plunger to the update worker to get the point, where all pendings in channel are skipped.
4. After plunger response, it's save to lock WAL (but still keep update lock)

After this steps, it's safe to call truncation in WAL, which is also trivial.

## Plunger usage safety
At first glance, plunger usage might cause a deadlock when the update worker has `Stop` operation. In this case, it's easy to expect that the plunger sender is not consumed and the receiver will wait forever.

But this easy test show that if `oneshot::Sender` is destroyed, the connected receiver is not blocked and returns `RecvError`:

```rust
#[tokio::test]
async fn test_update_signal_plugrer_drop() {
    let (sender, receiver) = channel::<UpdateSignal>(10);
        
    let (plunger_tx, plunger_rx) = oneshot::channel();
    let plunger = UpdateSignal::Plunger(plunger_tx);
    sender.send(plunger).await.unwrap();

    // Simulate stopping the update worker by dropping the receiver.
    let handler = tokio::spawn(async move {
        std::thread::sleep(std::time::Duration::from_millis(100));
        drop(receiver);    
    });

    // Check that the plunger finished even it is not explicitly plunged.
    let plunger_result = plunger_rx.await;
    println!("plunger result: {:?}", plunger_result);
    assert!(plunger_result.is_err());

    handler.await.unwrap();
}
```
This test is not included because it's a tokio test; there is no qdrant code usage here.

