```

I'd expect the "HELLO WORLD" to be printed after ~1 second, no panics to happen, and then the app to exit. I.e. The tokio-runtime should wait for the spawn-blocking task to end before shutting down, and the tokio sleep should work until the tokio-runtime has shutdown.

Instead, it prints this:

```
thread 'tokio-runtime-worker' panicked at 'A Tokio 1.x context was found, but it is being shutdown.', /playground/.cargo/registry/src/github.com-1ecc6299db9ec823/tokio-1.19.2/src/time/driver/entry.rs:551:13
note: [run with `RUST_BACKTRACE=1` environment variable to display a backtrace](https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=stable&mode=release&edition=2021#)
```

**Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.**

As in [the example of `AsyncFd`](https://docs.rs/tokio/1.28.2/tokio/io/unix/struct.AsyncFd.html#examples), it is quite tedious to impl `Async{Read,Write}` on top of it.

> ```rust
> impl AsyncRead for AsyncTcpStream {
>     fn poll_read(
>         self: Pin<&mut Self>,
>         cx: &mut Context<'_>,
>         buf: &mut ReadBuf<'_>
>     ) -> Poll<io::Result<()>> {
>         loop {
>             let mut guard = ready!(self.inner.poll_read_ready(cx))?;
> 
>             let unfilled = buf.initialize_unfilled();
>             match guard.try_io(|inner| inner.get_ref().read(unfilled)) {
>                 Ok(Ok(len)) => {
>                     buf.advance(len);
>                     return Poll::Ready(Ok(()));
>                 },
>                 Ok(Err(err)) => return Poll::Ready(Err(err)),
>                 Err(_would_block) => continue,
>             }
>         }
>     }
> }
> ```

`AsyncFd` is used for registering external fds to the tokio runtime. It's usual to `read` from it when it's `readable`, but awkwardly we only have [`poll_read_ready`](https://docs.rs/tokio/1.28.2/tokio/io/unix/struct.AsyncFd.html#method.poll_read_ready) but no `poll_read`. So does `poll_write`.

Implementing `Async{Read,Write}` can also make it easier import foreign fd-like object with async read and/or write supports into tokio ecosystem, by simply wrapping them in `AsyncFd`. Currently, we need to define a newtype struct and manually implements a complex poll-read/poll-write loop just like above.

**Describe the solution you'd like**

Since `AsyncFd` itself is a low-level API and we already have to trust user's `AsRawFd` impl, we can transfer the responsibility of the correctness of `Read`/`Write` impl to users: they must be non-blocking and return `WouldBlock` when it is no longer ready. If that's not held, it may block the runtime or lose readiness notifications, but no memory safety issues involved.

```rust
impl<T: AsRawFd + Read> AsyncRead for AsyncFd<T>;
impl<T: AsRawFd + Write> AsyncWrite for AsyncFd<T>;

impl<T: AsRawFd> AsyncRead for &AsyncFd<T> where for<'a> &'a T: Read;
impl<T: AsRawFd> AsyncWrite for &AsyncFd<T> where for<'a> &'a T: Write;
```

**Additional context**

- [`async_io::Async`](https://docs.rs/async-io/1.13.0/async_io/struct.Async.html) implements `Read` and `Write` when the inner type implements them.
- Private type `PollEvented` implements `Async{Read,Write}` and does a same job as `AsyncFd`. Could we somehow merge `PollEvented` and `AsyncFd`?

